          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What follow-up interval does guidance describe after starting a new blood pressure medicine?",
          "answer": "After starting a new blood pressure medication, the guidance recommends reassessment at roughly monthly intervals until blood pressure targets are reached. Once the readings become stable, reviews should occur every three to six months.\n\nThis information comes from two sources: the \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\" and \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\" guidelines. They both emphasize the importance of regular follow-up during the initial treatment phase.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
